I can help with that. Below is a focused checklist of what to ask your attorney and what evidence/documents to gather before the next hearing, plus a short email template you can send to Robert Kim. Tell me if you want me to draft or send the email for you.

What to ask your attorney (priority questions)
- What is the specific next court date and all hard deadlines (filings, exhibits, witness lists, disclosures)?  
- Based on the current record, do we need any additional evidence to meet our legal standard (burden of proof/defense)? If so, what exactly?  
- Which items of evidence will make the biggest difference at the hearing (documents, witness testimony, expert opinions)?  
- Are any outstanding discovery responses or productions still necessary from either side? Do we need to move to compel or to supplement our responses?  
- Should we schedule depositions (opposing party, witnesses, experts) before the hearing? If yes, who and when?  
- Do we need an expert (valuation, forensic accountant, vocational, medical) and how long will it take to obtain a usable report?  
- Are there any admissibility issues we should prepare for now (authentication of documents, hearsay, chain of custody)?  
- Should we prepare pretrial motions (motion in limine, summary judgment, evidentiary motions)? What are the pros/cons?  
- What should our exhibit list and witness list look like? When and how must those be exchanged with opposing counsel/court?  
- Are there settlement options we should explore, or is it better to litigate fully? If settlement, what is your recommended range/strategy?  
- Do you recommend any immediate preservation steps (spoliation letter, forensic imaging, preserving emails/texts)?  
- Are we compliant with all court rules and local practices for this hearing? Any administrative tasks I need to complete?  
- What should I do between now and the hearing to stay organized and prepared?

Documents and evidence to gather now
- Contract(s), offer letters, amendments, and any signed written agreements relevant to the dispute.  
- Emails and text messages between you and the former employer or creditor (search and collect full message threads).  
- Payroll records, invoices, payment receipts, bank statements, cancelled checks, and accounting records showing payments or debts.  
- Termination notices, disciplinary records, performance reviews, HR correspondence, company policies/manuals that relate to the dispute.  
- Any written demands, collection notices, account statements from the creditor.  
- Prior discovery responses, interrogatories, document productions, and any deposition transcripts.  
- Witness names and contact info; brief notes about what each witness can testify to.  
- Any photographs, signed statements/affidavits, contracts with third parties, or other physical evidence.  
- Logs or notes you’ve kept (dates, times, people involved) — convert them to a clean, dated chronology.  
- Expert contact info and existing reports (if any).  
- Court filings and procedural history (complaint, answers, motions, orders, proof of service).

Practical next steps you can take right away
- Create a chronological timeline of events (one page, bullet timeline) to share with your lawyer.  
- Secure and back up all relevant electronic communications and documents (save PDFs, export email threads).  
- Identify and list potential witnesses and their contact details. Note availability constraints.  
- Flag any documents that may need authentication and note their origin.  
- Ask your attorney about issuing preservation letters or subpoenas if needed.  
- If discovery is incomplete, ask your attorney to confirm whether to demand compliance or file a motion to compel.  
- Set a calendar reminder for each court deadline and ask your attorney to confirm deadlines in writing.

Short email template to your attorney (Robert Kim)
Subject: Next steps — Do we need additional evidence before upcoming hearing?

Hi Robert —

I’m writing to confirm next steps and to ask whether we need to gather additional evidence before the upcoming hearing in the contract dispute case.

Quick background: discovery recently closed and we are awaiting the hearing date. I want to be ready and make sure nothing is missing.

Could you please confirm:
1) The exact next court date and any immediate filing/exhibit/witness deadlines.  
2) Whether you recommend gathering any additional documents, depositions, or an expert report before the hearing — and if so, which items are highest priority.  
