Yes — I can help. Below are focused questions to ask your attorney about whether you need more evidence before the next hearing, a short checklist of things to gather, and a short email template I can draft/send to your lawyer (Robert Kim) if you want.

Questions to ask your attorney
- Do we currently have enough admissible evidence to prove each element of my claims/defenses? If not, which elements are weak?
- Which specific documents or exhibits would strengthen our position (contracts, emails/texts, invoices, payment records, bank statements, time-stamped logs)?
- Are there witnesses whose testimony would materially help? If so, what should I ask them and how should I document their statements?
- Do you recommend an expert (accountant, damages expert, etc.)? If yes, what deadlines and costs are involved?
- Were there any discovery responses the opposing side gave that point to missing evidence we should obtain?
- Are there outstanding discovery requests we should serve (or objections to challenge) now?
- Do we need subpoenas for records (bank, phone carrier, employers) or depositions? If so, what’s the timeline to get them?
- Are there any evidentiary or chain-of-custody concerns about documents I already have? Do they need notarization, affidavits, or metadata preservation?
- What are the deadline(s) I must meet (motions, disclosures, expert reports)? What happens if we miss them?
- Do you recommend we move for any pretrial motions now (summary judgment, exclusion of evidence, sanctions)?
- What is the likely effect of waiting until the hearing vs obtaining more evidence now (strategy/risk)?
- What is the estimated cost and time to obtain the additional evidence you recommend?
- If we obtain new evidence, how long until it can be served and used in this case?
- Any confidentiality or privilege concerns with documents we plan to collect or share?
- Is settlement still an option and would additional evidence change its value?

Practical evidence checklist (what you can pull together now)
- Chronological timeline of events with dates and short descriptions.
- All written agreements/contracts and any amendments.
- All emails and text messages (export or screenshot with timestamps).
- Bank statements, cancelled checks, payment records, invoices, receipts.
- Call logs and voicemail screenshots where applicable.
- Personnel records or employment documents if employment-related.
- Photos, video, or other media — preserve originals and note metadata.
- Names, contact info, and short summaries for potential witnesses.
- Any prior court filings, discovery responses, and the opposing party’s disclosures.
- Notes of in-person conversations (date/time, participants, summary).

How I can help now
