I pulled together a focused checklist and a short set of questions you can ask Robert Kim to decide whether you need more evidence before the next step. I also wrote a ready-to-send email you can use (or I can draft/save/send it for you if you want — I’ll need Robert’s email address).

Top questions to ask your attorney (prioritized)
- What is the exact response/deadline I must meet now (date and required filing)? Do we have time to collect additional evidence before that deadline?  
- Given the current status (discovery closed, waiting for hearing), do you expect any pre-hearing motions or additional discovery we should prepare for?  
- Based on strengths/weaknesses of our current evidence, what specific types of additional evidence would materially help our position? (e.g., contracts, emails, pay stubs, timecards, witness statements)  
- Do you recommend obtaining sworn witness statements or affidavits now, or wait until closer to the hearing?  
- Are there any evidence preservation steps I should take immediately (save copies of emails, screenshots of messages, preserve devices, back up accounts)?  
- Could additional evidence trigger new disclosure obligations or open us to counter-discovery? What are the costs/benefits?  
- Do you want me to locate and provide any particular documents now? If so, what format and how should I transmit them?  
- Do we need expert witnesses or an investigator to collect anything? If so, what’s the timeline and cost estimate?  
- What are the likely next procedural dates and our strategy for them (motion schedule, settlement talks, mediation)?  
- Are there any risks with collecting more evidence (privacy issues, spoliation risk, admissibility concerns)?

Practical evidence checklist to gather (if relevant)
- Signed contracts, amendments, offer letters, termination notices
- All email chains and attachments related to the dispute (both sides)
- Text messages, instant messages, Slack/Teams conversations (screenshots + originals if possible)
- Pay stubs, invoices, payment records, bank statements showing disputed amounts
- Time sheets, calendars, performance reviews, disciplinary records
- Confirmation of deliveries, receipts, invoices, purchase orders
- Any written policies or employee handbooks relevant to the dispute
- Names and contact info for potential witnesses and short descriptions of what each witnessed
- Any recordings, voicemails, or photographs (preserve originals)
- Any previously produced discovery or responses you received from opposing party

Immediate steps to take now
- Preserve all potentially relevant communications and documents (don’t delete anything). Make backups.  
- Make a short written timeline of events with dates and what happened — this helps your attorney spot gaps.  
- List potential witnesses and a one-sentence summary of what each can testify to.  
- Ask your attorney if a short supplemental production is advisable and whether you should get sworn statements now.

Short sample email to Robert Kim (ready to send)
Subject: Quick: documents & next steps before response/hearing

Hi Robert — hope you’re well. Quick update and a few questions as we wait for a hearing date in the contract dispute case:

1) Can you confirm the exact deadline I must meet now (response/filing deadline) and whether we have time to collect additional evidence before then?  
2) Based on where we are (discovery just closed), do you recommend gathering any additional documents or sworn witness statements now? If so, which items would be most useful?  
3) Are there any immediate preservation steps I should take beyond saving emails/messages?  
4) Would you like me to send you a folder of documents (contracts, emails, pay records, timeline, witness list)? If yes, preferred format and secure method?

I’ve started a timeline and a list of potential witnesses I can share. Let me know if you want to schedule a short call this week to go over priorities.

Thanks,  
[Your name]  
[Best phone number]

Would you like me to:
- Draft and save this to your drafts for review?
- Send it now to Robert (if you give his email)?
- Or just refine the email (shorter/longer or more formal)?

If you want, tell me Robert’s email and whether you want me to send or draft it. Also tell me any dates/deadlines you already know so I can customize the email.

