Thanks — I can help with this. I put together a focused checklist of questions you can ask your attorney about whether you need more evidence before the next hearing, plus next-step suggestions. I can also draft an email to Robert Kim (or save it as a draft) and/or look up his contact in your inbox if you want.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney
- What is the exact next court date/deadline and what will happen at that hearing? (What decisions will the judge likely make?)
- Are there outstanding discovery items (documents, responses, depositions) we still need to obtain or produce?
- Based on the current record, is more evidence necessary to meet our burden or to defeat the creditor’s claims? If so, what specific evidence would be most helpful?
- Which documents or records should I start gathering now (bank statements, contracts, emails, invoices, payment records, text messages)?
- Do we need any witness statements or declarations? If so, who should we ask and what should they say?
- Would an expert (accountant, handwriting, valuation, etc.) strengthen our position — and is an expert feasible before the hearing?
- Should we subpoena documents or witnesses? If yes, what’s the timeline and cost?
- Are there potential evidentiary problems with what we already have (authenticity, chain of custody, hearsay, missing foundation)?
- Do you recommend taking any depositions before the hearing? If so, who and about what?
- What is our plan for exhibits at the hearing (which documents to mark and how to present them)?
- Are there strategic reasons not to obtain more evidence (cost, delay, risk of revealing weaknesses)?
- Do we have any procedural options that could buy time (continuance, motion practice) if more evidence is needed?
- What documents should I bring to our next meeting or to the hearing itself?
- Are settlement negotiations appropriate now? If so, what terms should I be prepared to accept/seek?
- What are the likely outcomes and how will additional evidence change those probabilities or remedies?

Next-step suggestions I can do for you
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions (I can send it or save as a draft).
- Search your inbox for emails from/to Robert Kim or Kim & Associates so we can reply or forward relevant documents.
- Help you organize and label the documents to collect (create a checklist you can use).
- Draft short witness declaration templates or a subpoena request to review with your attorney.

How would you like to proceed?
- Want me to draft an email to Robert Kim now? If yes: do you want it sent immediately, or saved as a draft? Also tell me if you prefer a formal or conversational tone and whether to include any case details (hearing date, case number).
- Or should I search your inbox for Robert Kim’s email and recent correspondence first?

Pick one and I’ll take the next step.

