Yes — I can help. Below are focused questions to ask Robert Kim and a short checklist of evidence/documents to gather. I also included a ready-to-send email you can use or I can draft/send for you if you want.

Key questions to ask your attorney (prioritize these first)
- Procedural / Deadlines
  - What is the exact next court date and filing deadline(s) I need to meet now?
  - Do I need to file any immediate responses or motions before the next date?
  - Are there local rules or specific procedures in Maricopa County Housing Court I should be aware of?

- Evidence / Discovery status
  - Based on what we already have from discovery, are there any critical gaps in the evidence?
  - Which documents or records would most strengthen our position (contracts, emails, payment records, text messages, call logs, witness statements)?
  - Do you recommend subpoenas for documents or testimony (bank records, employer records, third parties)? If so, who should be subpoenaed and by when?

- Witnesses and testimony
  - Which witnesses (if any) should we prepare or consider calling? Are written witness statements or declarations helpful?
  - Do we need an expert witness? If so, on what topics and what is the process/cost/timeline?

- Admissibility & preservation
  - Are there any authentication or chain-of-custody concerns for key documents (e.g., digital files, metadata)?
  - Do we need to preserve or secure anything immediately (freeze accounts, save emails, preserve devices)?

- Strategy & risk
  - What are the strongest and weakest parts of our case given the discovery record?
  - Would you recommend settlement discussions now, or wait until after the next hearing?
  - What motions (e.g., motion to dismiss, summary judgment, motion in limine) should we consider and what is the likelihood of success?

- Logistics & costs
  - Roughly what will it cost and how long will it take to obtain additional evidence or experts?
  - What can I reasonably do myself vs. what you will handle?
  - What is the preferred way to communicate updates (email/phone) and typical turnaround time?

Quick checklist of documents to gather now
- All contracts, amendments, and signed documents related to the dispute
- All emails and text messages between you and the former employer/creditor
- Payment records, invoices, bank statements, payroll records
- Any performance reviews, termination notices, HR communications
- Receipts, timestamps, or logs that corroborate your timeline
- Names and contact info for potential witnesses
- Any notices, court papers, or prior correspondence from the opposing party
- Screenshots/backups of any online communications or posts

Sample email you can send to Robert Kim
