Thanks — I see you’re in a contract-dispute matter with Robert Kim at Kim & Associates and you’ve just finished discovery and are waiting for a hearing date. Do you want: (A) a list of questions to ask your attorney now, (B) a draft email to Robert Kim you can send, or (C) both? If you want me to draft or send an email I’ll need the attorney’s email address (or I can save a draft).

Below are quick, practical items you can use now — a focused set of questions to ask Robert and a checklist of evidence to consider gathering/preserving.

Questions to ask your attorney
- What is the exact next court deadline(s) and hearing date(s) we should be preparing for? (When is our deadline to file anything further?)
- Based on discovery so far, do you think we need additional evidence to strengthen our position? If so, what specifically?
- Are there witnesses we should interview or ask to testify? Do any need subpoenas?
- Should we seek additional written discovery, document subpoenas, or depositions before the hearing?
- Are there documents/emails/text messages I should preserve immediately? Any custodians I should notify to preserve records?
- Do we need expert witness(s) (e.g., forensic accountant, contract expert)? If so, when should we retain them?
- What motions or pre-hearing filings do you recommend (motions in limine, summary judgment, evidentiary challenges)?
- What is the likely timeline and cost for obtaining additional evidence or expert reports?
- What strengths/weaknesses do you see in the opponent’s case and in ours based on discovery?
- What are the settlement options and when should we consider settlement discussions?
- What should I do now to prepare for the hearing (organize documents, timeline, witness statements)?
- Is there anything time-sensitive I need to do immediately (file responses, preserve ESI, serve subpoenas)?

Evidence/documents to preserve or gather now
- All written communications with the former employer/creditor: emails, texts, letters, DMs
- Contracts, amendments, offer letters, payment records, invoices, bank statements
- Time-stamped logs, work records, project files, deliverables, internal notes
- Performance reviews, HR communications, termination notices, policy docs
- Records of payments, reimbursements, expense reports
- Any witness contact info and short written statements or notes of what they would say
- Backups, metadata, and original files for digital documents (don’t delete anything)
- Calendar entries and meeting notes
- Phone call logs or voicemail records, if relevant

Immediate practical steps
- Preserve all relevant ESI (don’t delete emails/texts); back up copies
- Make a timeline of key events with dates and supporting documents
- List potential witnesses with contact details and short summaries of what they know
- If needed, authorize retention of an expert quickly (ask attorney about costs/turnaround)
- Confirm with your attorney whether any filings or responses are due soon

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for his guidance (I’ll need his email or I can save a draft), or
- Draft a more detailed memo you can bring to your next meeting, or
- Create a checklist template you can use to collect documents and witness statements.

Which would you like me to do next?

