Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help with that. Below is a focused checklist of questions to ask Robert Kim (or bring up at your next call/meeting), a list of documents/evidence to gather now, and a short next-steps plan. If you’d like, I can also draft an email to your lawyer using this checklist.

Priority questions to ask your attorney
- Case status & timeline
  - What is the next scheduled date (hearing/trial/settlement conference) and what must be filed before then?
  - Are there any immediate deadlines I need to be aware of (responses, motions, exhibit lists, witness lists, pretrial statements)?
- Evidence & completeness
  - Based on discovery so far, do you feel we have enough admissible evidence to meet our goals at the next hearing?
  - What specific pieces of additional evidence would strengthen our position (emails, contracts, invoices, payment records, witnesses, receipts)?
  - Are there any gaps or weaknesses in the evidence the opposing side could exploit?
- Witnesses & testimony
  - Do we need any witness statements or depositions before the next date? If so, who should be deposed or contacted?
  - Should we issue subpoenas for documents or witnesses? If yes, what’s the timeline for that?
- Admissibility & format
  - Are there any concerns about admissibility for the documents we have (authentication, hearsay, chain of custody)?
  - Do exhibits need to be organized/numbered and served in advance? What format does the court prefer?
- Motions & strategy
  - Should we file or respond to any motions now (e.g., motion to compel, motion in limine)?
  - What is our overall strategy for the next hearing — seek settlement, seek continuance, push for summary judgment, etc.?
- Costs & logistics
  - What are estimated costs and time needed to secure additional evidence (private investigator, expert, subpoena fees, deposition costs)?
  - If we gather more evidence, will it materially delay the case or is there time to do it before the next date?
- Contingency planning
  - If we can’t obtain additional evidence in time, what’s the backup plan at the hearing?
  - Are there steps I should take personally to preserve evidence (save emails, screenshots, physical items)?
- Communication & action items
  - What do you need from me right now, and by when?
  - How should I send documents to you (secure portal, email, physical copies)?

Documents and items to gather now
- Original contracts, amendments, signed pages, and any written terms
- All emails, text messages, and messaging app communications relevant to the dispute
- Payment records: invoices, canceled checks, bank statements, credit card statements, receipts
- Time logs or work records (if relevant)
- Any discovery responses already exchanged, plus deposition transcripts
- Notices, demand letters, service documents, court filings you’ve received or filed
- Contact details for potential witnesses and a short summary of what each can testify about
- Photos, recordings, or any physical evidence; notes about chain of custody
- A one-page chronological timeline of events (dates, short description, supporting doc references)

Practical next steps (what you can do right now)
- Create a one-page chronology of the case with document references (helps your attorney spot gaps quickly)
- Back up and organize all evidence into a single folder (digital and scanned copies), label each file with date and short description
- Preserve original documents and avoid deleting any relevant messages or files
- Make a list of potential witnesses with contact info and short notes about what they observed
- Decide whether you want to ask your attorney for an in-person or phone prep session before the hearing
- Ask your attorney for a prioritized to-do list with deadlines

Short email template you can send to your attorney
Subject: Questions & Documents re: [Case name or case number] — next hearing on [date]

Hi Robert —  
Our next court date is on [date]. Before then I’d like to confirm what additional evidence (if any) we should collect and what you need from me. Specifically:
- Do we have enough admissible evidence for the hearing, or should I gather additional documents/witnesses? If so, what exactly?  
- Are there any filing deadlines or motions I should be aware of and prepare for?  
- Do you recommend depositions or subpoenas at this stage?  
I’ve attached a one-page chronology and the documents I currently have. Please let me know a good time to talk this week or whether you prefer I send more materials via the client portal.  
Thanks,  
[Your name]  
(attach chronology and key docs)

Would you like me to:
- Draft the full email and save it as a draft or send it now?
- Organize a checklist/digital folder template you can use to collect the documents?
- Prepare a short script or bullet points for your call with Robert so you cover everything efficiently?

Note: I’m not your lawyer — these are organizational and preparatory suggestions. Your attorney will advise on legal strategy and what is legally necessary.

