Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I saw your memory about the contract-dispute case with Robert Kim (Kim & Associates) in Maricopa County. Below is a focused checklist of questions you can ask your attorney to determine whether you need more evidence before the next date, plus a short sample email you can send (or I can draft/send for you if you want).

Questions to ask your attorney (organized & prioritized)
- Case status & deadlines
  - What is the exact date and purpose of the next court appearance (hearing, status conference, pretrial)?
  - Are there any filing deadlines (motions, exhibits, witness lists, pretrial disclosures) I must meet before that date?
  - What is the likely timeline for next steps after that hearing?

- Discovery & evidence sufficiency
  - Is discovery formally closed? If not, what remains outstanding?
  - Based on what we already have, do you consider our evidence sufficient to survive any summary judgment motion or to present at the hearing?
  - What specific pieces of evidence would strengthen our position most (documents, emails, contracts, payment records, phone logs, metadata)?
  - Are there gaps or inconsistencies in the opponent’s evidence we should exploit? If yes, how should we obtain confirming evidence?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Do we need any witness declarations or live testimony at the hearing? Who should we call?
  - Should we seek depositions or statements from any witnesses (former coworkers, managers, third parties)?
  - Do any witnesses need subpoenas or prepared affidavits?

- Requests and subpoenas
  - Should we issue subpoenas for records (bank records, employer records, phone company records)? If so, for what specifically?
  - Can we seek non-party documents quickly if needed? How long do those subpoenas take to fulfill?

- Expert evidence
  - Would an expert (accountant, industry expert) help our case? If so, what would they need to prepare and how long would that take?
  - Would the cost/time be justified given the case stage?

- Motions & strategy
  - Are you planning any motions (motion in limine, summary judgment, motion to compel) before the hearing?
  - If the other side files a motion, what evidence would we need to oppose it effectively?
  - What is our best approach at the upcoming hearing (settlement-focused, evidentiary, procedural)?

- Document checklist & format
  - Which documents should I gather now (original contracts, emails, invoices, payment confirmations, time-stamped logs)?
  - Do you need originals, certified copies, or will digital/photocopies suffice? Should anything be authenticated?

- Logistics & responsibilities
  - What do you want me to collect personally vs. what you’ll request/obtain through formal discovery?
  - Are there forms or authorizations I should sign (consent to obtain records)?
  - Who will prepare exhibit lists and organize trial binders?

- Costs & timing trade-offs
  - Rough estimate of costs/time for extra discovery (depositions, subpoenas, expert).
  - Given the hearing date, is it better to focus on immediate evidence we can get quickly or to seek more extensive discovery later?

- Contingencies & settlement
  - What are our realistic settlement options based on current evidence?
  - If we need more time to obtain critical evidence, what are the options for requesting a continuance and the likelihood the court will grant it?

Quick document checklist to start collecting now
- Original signed contract(s) and amendments
- Emails and texts relating to the dispute (with dates)
- Invoices, payment records, bank statements, cancelled checks
- Work product showing performance or non-performance (time logs, deliverables)
- Communications with the opposing party (demand letters, notices)
- Any prior court filings, discovery responses, deposition transcripts
- IDs, employment records, or other corroborating third-party records

Sample brief email to Robert Kim (you can send as-is or I can draft/send it)
Subject: Questions before next hearing — [Your Name] v. [Opposing Party] (Maricopa County)

Body:
Hi Robert,

I want to make sure we’re fully prepared for the upcoming date. Before our next hearing, could you confirm the exact purpose and any filing deadlines? Also please advise whether discovery is closed and whether you think we need additional evidence (specific documents, witness statements, depositions, or subpoenas). If you recommend further collection, please let me know what you want me to gather personally and what you’ll request through formal discovery, plus an estimate of time and cost. Finally, are you planning any motions or strategic steps I should be aware of?

Thanks — I want to be ready and can start collecting documents right away.

Best,
[Your name]

Next steps
- Do you want me to draft this email as a saved draft or send it now to Robert Kim? If send, please confirm the attorney email address (or say “use the address on file”).
- I can also adapt the questions for a short checklist you can bring to a call/meeting with him.
- Would you like a prioritized list of the top 3 documents to gather immediately?

Tell me how you want to proceed and I’ll prepare the email or checklist.

