I can help with that. Below are (A) a focused checklist of questions to ask your attorney about whether you need more evidence before the next date, (B) a short evidence/document checklist to prepare, and (C) a short sample email you can send to your attorney (Robert Kim) asking these things. Tell me if you want me to draft or send the email for you.

A — Key questions to ask your attorney
- Case status & deadlines
  - What is the exact next court date and filing deadlines I must meet before then?
  - Are there any imminent deadlines for filing motions, exhibits, or responses I should know about?
- Evidence sufficiency
  - Based on what we already have, do you think we have enough evidence to meet our goals at the next hearing?
  - What specific pieces of evidence would strengthen our position (documents, receipts, emails, texts, photos, bank records, contracts)?
- Missing evidence & how to get it
  - Are there particular documents or witnesses we should subpoena or depose now?
  - Should we send preservation or records requests to third parties (banks, former employer, vendor)?
  - If we need expert support (e.g., accounting, valuations), when should we retain them?
- Authentication & admissibility
  - Do any documents need notarization, certification, or an affidavit to be admissible?
  - Are there chain-of-custody or authenticity concerns for any evidence we plan to use?
- Discovery and motions
  - Should we seek additional discovery (interrogatories, requests for production, depositions)? If so, what should be the focus?
  - Do you recommend any pre-hearing motions (motion in limine, summary judgment, protective orders)?
- Strategy & settlement
  - What is our strategy at the upcoming date (bench trial, hearing, status conference, settlement discussion)?
  - Would additional evidence help our settlement leverage or is settlement likely regardless?
- Logistics & preparation
  - How should I organize exhibits for the court (numbering, index, copies)?
  - Do you want originals, certified copies, or electronic versions? How many sets?
