Yes — I can help. Below are focused questions to ask your attorney about whether you need more evidence before the next hearing, a short checklist of documents/evidence to gather, recommended next steps, and a ready-to-send email you can use to contact Robert Kim. Tell me which action you want next (draft email, send email, search your inbox for messages from Robert, or just save these notes).

Questions to ask your attorney
- Current status & deadlines
  - What is the exact next court date and what will happen at that hearing?
  - Are there any filing or disclosure deadlines I must meet before the hearing?
- Case posture & strategy
  - Based on what we’ve already exchanged in discovery, do you see any clear gaps in our evidence?
  - What’s our strategy for the hearing (motion, status, trial-setting, settlement)?
- Evidence needs
  - Which specific facts do we need stronger proof of, and what type of evidence would be most persuasive (documents, emails, bank records, witness testimony, expert report)?
  - Do we need affidavits or declarations from witnesses? If so, who should be asked and what should they say?
  - Is an expert witness likely needed? If so, what kind and how long to retain one?
- Discovery & subpoenas
  - Should we subpoena any documents or witnesses (former employer, creditor, third parties like banks)?
  - Are there outstanding discovery responses we should compel or follow up on?
- Authentication & admissibility
  - Are there authentication or chain-of-custody issues with any evidence we already have (emails, digital records)?
  - Will any documents require foundation or witness testimony to be admitted?
- Risk, costs & timing
  - What are the costs and time needed to obtain additional evidence vs. proceeding with what we have?
  - If we need more time, how likely is the court to grant a continuance?
- Settlement posture
  - Given our evidence so far, what’s the realistic settlement outlook and timing for settlement discussions?
- Action items for you
  - What specific documents or actions should I prioritize collecting in the next X days?
  - Do you want me to prepare witness summaries/affidavits or help draft subpoenas?

Documents and evidence checklist to gather
- Employment-related: employment contract, offer letter, termination letter, performance reviews, disciplinary notices
- Communications: all emails, texts, Slack or other messages with employer/creditor, and dates/times
- Financial: pay stubs, bank statements, invoices, receipts relevant to claimed damages or payments
- Discovery materials: all discovery responses already produced (ours and theirs), deposition transcripts, interrogatory answers, production indices
- Third-party records: communications or records from banks, payroll, vendors, clients
- Witness information: names, contact info, short summary of what each witness can testify to
- Records of service/court filings: proof of service, filed motions, court notices
- Any contemporaneous notes or diary entries about incidents or meetings

Practical next steps you can take now
- Organize and label all documents by topic/date so your attorney can review quickly.
- Make short summaries (1 paragraph) for each witness and what they can confirm.
- Identify any likely third-party witnesses and whether they can be subpoenaed.
- Preserve evidence: don’t delete relevant emails/messages or devices; consider a preservation letter if needed.
- Decide whether you want to request a brief meeting (phone/Zoom) with your attorney to go through the list.

