Yes — I can help. Below is a focused list of questions to ask your attorney about whether you need more evidence before the next date, a short checklist of documents/evidence to gather, and a short sample email you can send to Robert Kim (I can draft/save/send it for you if you want — tell me his email and whether you want it sent now).

Key questions to ask your lawyer
- What is our immediate deadline (response, motion, or other filing) and the hearing date? Are any calendar dates I need to know about now?
- Based on the discovery we already received, is the current record strong enough for our intended defense/response? What gaps do you see?
- What specific types of evidence would be most helpful to strengthen our position (documents, witnesses, bank records, communications, photos, metadata, expert report, affidavits)?
- Do we need any additional discovery (supplemental requests, interrogatories, requests for admission, subpoenas for records or depositions)? If so, what should we request and on what timeline?
- Are there witnesses we should get signed declarations from now? Which witnesses are highest priority?
- Do you recommend hiring an expert (e.g., forensic accountant, vocational expert)? If so, what is the scope and estimated timing/cost?
- Do we need to preserve or secure any evidence immediately (digital backups, phone records, cloud data)? Any steps I should take to avoid spoliation?
- Are there privilege, confidentiality, or authentication concerns with the documents we plan to use? Anything we should redact or avoid sharing?
- If the creditor files a motion (summary judgment, default, etc.), what additional evidence would counter it?
- What is our settlement posture right now — should I be prepared to exchange settlement offers or avoid certain communications?
- What are the likely next procedural steps and estimated timeline to hearing/trial? What should I expect at the next court date?
- What will you need from me this week (copies, witness contacts, authorization forms, retainer for experts)?
- What are the estimated costs and billing implications for additional discovery or expert work?

Document/evidence checklist to gather now
- Contracts, agreements, amendments, or written terms relevant to the dispute
- All written communications (emails, texts, direct messages) with the former employer/creditor and related third parties
- Invoices, receipts, bank statements, canceled checks, payroll records, or billing records
- Demand letters, collection notices, pleadings, discovery responses, and any court filings already exchanged
- Proof of service and any correspondence about deadlines or notices
- A chronology/timeline of events (dates, short descriptions, supporting doc IDs)
- Witness list with contact info + short summary of what each witness can confirm
- Photos, screenshots, call logs, metadata, or other digital evidence (export copies if possible)
- Any previously produced discovery from the other side (save copies) and notes where you think it’s incomplete or inconsistent
- Any settlement offers, ADR communications, or notes of conversations about settlement

Practical next actions (short-term)
- Preserve all relevant digital files and create a simple folder structure (e.g., Contracts / Communications / Financial / Court Documents / Witnesses).
- Export and save text threads and emails as PDFs (don’t rely on screenshots alone).
- Make a concise written timeline (one page) you can give to your attorney.
- Identify 1–3 priority witnesses and ask if they can prepare brief written statements or be available for a call.
- Ask your attorney whether to avoid direct contact with the creditor or opposing counsel (some communications can hurt your case).

