Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — based on your legal-memory note, I put together a focused checklist of questions you can ask your attorney (Robert Kim) about whether you need more evidence before the next hearing, plus a short sample email you can send. Tell me if you want me to draft or send this email for you.

Questions to ask your attorney before the next hearing
- Case status & timeline
  - What is the exact next court date and filing deadlines I must meet?
  - Are there any upcoming deadlines for exchanging exhibits, witness lists, or pretrial submissions?
- Evidence sufficiency
  - Based on discovery so far, do we have enough admissible evidence to support our key arguments?
  - Which claims/defenses are strongest and which need more support?
- Specific documents to gather
  - Are there specific documents you want me to find (contracts, emails, invoices, bank statements, payment records, call logs, receipts)?
  - Do you need contemporaneous notes, dated calendars, or screenshots?
- Witnesses & statements
  - Which witnesses would be most helpful? Do we need written witness statements or affidavits now?
  - Should we prepare witnesses for deposition or hearing testimony?
- Expert evidence
  - Do we need an expert (e.g., forensic accountant, contract expert)? If so, when should we retain one?
- Admissibility & authentication
  - Any documents that might face authenticity or hearsay objections? How should we authenticate them?
  - Do we need chain-of-custody proof or certified copies for any records?
- Discovery follow-up
  - Are there outstanding discovery requests or motions I should push for (e.g., subpoenas, document requests)?
  - Should we seek court intervention for missing documents?
- Preservation & collection
  - Should I preserve any additional records or devices now (emails, phones, cloud storage)?
  - Any recommended steps to avoid spoliation (written preservation notice, copying files)?
- Tactical & settlement questions
  - What is the defense strategy for the hearing? Are there settlement options to explore before then?
  - How will admitting or withholding certain evidence affect the strategy?
- Logistics & costs
  - What are the expected costs/time to obtain additional evidence or experts?
  - Will gathering more evidence meaningfully improve our position for the hearing?
- Next steps & communication
  - What do you want me to prioritize this week and what exactly do you need from me by specific dates?
  - Can we schedule a quick call to review urgency and a checklist of items I should gather?

Short sample email to Robert Kim
Subject: Questions about evidence needed before next hearing — [Your Name]

Hi Robert,

We’re approaching the next hearing, and I want to make sure I’ve collected everything you need. Can you please confirm:
- The next court date and any imminent filing/exchange deadlines.
- Whether the documents we produced in discovery are sufficient, or if you want me to locate additional documents (contracts, emails, bank records, receipts, phone logs, etc.).
- Any witnesses you recommend we prepare or get written statements from.
- Whether we should retain an expert or issue any subpoenas.
