I see from your memory that you're working with Robert Kim at Kim & Associates on a contract dispute and want to be ready for the next legal steps. I can help you prepare questions to ask your attorney, draft an email to Robert Kim, or search your inbox for his messages. Which would you like me to do next?

Below are two quick things I can do right away plus a checklist you can use when speaking to your lawyer.

1) Suggested questions to ask your attorney (grouped)
- Case status & deadlines
  - What is the next court date/hearing and its purpose?
  - Are there any deadlines coming up (motions, disclosures, filings) I need to know about?
- Evidence & discovery
  - Is the discovery phase fully closed or can we still request documents/witness statements?
  - Do you think we need additional evidence to meet our burden or counter their claims?
  - Which documents/electronic records are most important for my case?
  - Do we need to subpoena records (bank, employer, phone carrier, etc.) or depose witnesses?
- Evidence handling & authenticity
  - How should I preserve and organize documents, emails, texts, receipts, logs, or other records?
  - Do we need to authenticate digital evidence (metadata, screenshots, original files)?
  - Should we prepare witness statements or affidavits now?
- Experts & witnesses
  - Would an expert (e.g., accountant) help our case? If so, when must they be identified?
  - Which witnesses are most helpful and when should we interview/prepare them?
- Motions & strategy
  - Should we file any pre-hearing motions (summary judgment, evidentiary, protective)?
  - What is our short-term strategy for the hearing and for settlement discussions?
- Practicalities & costs
  - What are the likely costs and timelines if we pursue additional discovery or experts?
  - Are there steps I can take personally to avoid hurting our position?
- Communications & next steps
  - How should I get you new documents? (email, secure portal, physical drop-off)
  - When can we have a short call or meeting to go over what I should gather next?

2) Specific documents & items to gather (start collecting these now)
- Contracts, amendments, written agreements
- All emails and text messages related to the dispute (include dates)
- Payment records, invoices, receipts, bank statements, payroll records
- Calendar entries, meeting notes, call logs
- Any written notices, demand letters, or court documents received
- Names and contact info of possible witnesses and short summaries of what they’d say
- Records showing damages or expenses you claim (if applicable)
- Any prior correspondence with the opposing party or their lawyer

3) Quick sample email to Robert Kim (I can send or save as draft)
Subject: Preparing evidence before next hearing / questions

Hi Robert — I want to be prepared for the next steps in the contract dispute. Could we confirm the next hearing date and any upcoming deadlines? Also, please advise whether we need additional discovery or subpoenas and which documents or witness statements I should gather now. If possible, can we schedule a short call this week to go over strategy and what I should collect? I can send over the documents I already have. Thanks, [Your Name]

Would you like me to:
- Draft and save that email to Drafts for you?
- Send it now to Robert Kim (I can send it immediately)?
- Search your inbox for prior emails from Robert Kim or about the case so you can reference them?
- Or would you prefer a printable checklist of evidence to bring to your meeting?

Tell me which action you want and any edits to the email (tone, details to add), and I’ll do it.

