Thanks — this is helpful context. Would you like: (A) a checklist of focused questions to ask your attorney about whether you need more evidence before the next hearing, (B) a ready-to-send/draft email to your lawyer Robert Kim asking those questions, or (C) both? I’ve prepared both below so you can pick.

Checklist — focused questions to ask Robert Kim
- What is the next court date and the deadline to disclose exhibits/witnesses?  
- Based on the discovery you’ve completed, do we currently have enough evidence to meet our burden/support our defenses? If not, what’s missing?  
- Which specific documents or types of records would strengthen our position (emails, contracts, payment records, call logs, text messages, bank records, invoices, receipts)?  
- Are there any witnesses whose statements or testimony we should get now? Should we take written declarations or depose them?  
- Do we need expert testimony or a report (e.g., damages calculation, accounting)? If so, what’s the timeline and cost?  
- Are there any outstanding discovery responses we should compel from the opposing party (motions to compel)?  
- Should we consider subpoenas for third-party records (banks, phone companies, employers)? What lead time is required?  
- Are there forensic or technical steps needed (e.g., recover deleted messages, preserve electronic evidence)?  
- What exhibits do you recommend preparing now and in what format (numbered, labeled, binder/PDF)?  
- What is the likely procedural posture at the hearing (settlement conference, motion hearing, trial setting)? How should evidence be presented?  
- Are there settlement options we should explore now, and would additional evidence materially change settlement leverage?  
- What are the risks/costs of gathering additional evidence versus relying on what we have?  
- Do you recommend a meeting to review evidence together before the deadline? If so, when?  
- What do you need from me immediately (documents, signed authorizations, written statements) and how should I deliver them?

Short email template to Robert Kim (ready to send or save as a draft)
Subject: Questions about evidence and next steps — upcoming hearing in [case name or court docket #]

Hi Robert —  
I hope you’re well. I wanted to confirm our next court date is [insert date]. With the hearing coming up, I’d like to make sure we have everything we need. Could we review whether additional evidence is necessary and what I should collect before the deadline?

Specifically:
- Do we currently have sufficient evidence for our position, or is anything missing?
- Which documents/witnesses should I prioritize collecting (examples: emails, bank records, texts, contracts)?
- Do we need any subpoenas, expert reports, or depositions? If so, what are the timelines and costs?
- Would it be helpful to meet (phone or in person) to go through exhibits? I’m available [insert 2–3 times/dates].

Please let me know what you recommend and what you need from me right away. Thanks — I want to be fully prepared.

Best,  
[Your name]  
[Phone number]

Next steps
- Tell me whether you want me to draft this as an email draft to send to Robert (I can save it to drafts) or send it now.  
- If yes, give me the hearing date and any specifics you want included (case name/docket number, two meeting times, your phone number) and whether to send or save as draft.

Would you like me to create the draft for you now?

