Thanks — I see from your memory that you’re in a contract-dispute case with Robert Kim (Kim & Associates) and you want to be ready for the next court date and figure out whether you need more evidence. I can help with that. Below are focused questions to ask your attorney plus a short email template you can send to Robert Kim. Tell me if you want me to draft or send the email, save it as a draft, or search your inbox for previous messages from Robert Kim first.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney
- What is the exact next court date (hearing/trial/briefing deadline) and all related deadlines I must meet? (Confirm dates and filing deadlines.)
- Based on where discovery ended, what specific legal issues will be decided at the next date? (Clarifies scope so you know what evidence matters.)
- Do you recommend obtaining any additional documents or records? If so, which ones and from whom? (Be specific: contracts, emails, invoices, call logs, bank records.)
- Are there any gaps in the evidence we currently have that might hurt our position? Which gaps are highest priority to fix?
- Do we need any witness statements or declarations? If yes, who should we approach and what should they say?
- Should we get expert testimony or a written expert report (e.g., accounting, contract interpretation)? If so, by when?
- Are there pending motions we should prepare (or oppose) before the hearing? What evidence supports those motions?
- What discovery remedies remain available if the opponent refuses to produce needed documents? (motions to compel, sanctions, subpoenas)
- Do you recommend subpoenaing third parties? If so, which ones and what document types should we subpoena?
- What is the standard of proof or legal test the court will apply at this stage? How does our evidence align with that standard?
- Can we prepare a concise trial/exchange binder or evidence summary for the lawyer and the court? What should be included?
- Do you see any credibility or authenticity issues with existing documents we should fix (e.g., metadata, notarization, chain of custody)?
- Are there settlement options to consider now that could avoid further discovery or trial? If yes, what’s the timeline to pursue them?
- What are the estimated costs/time to secure any additional evidence you recommend? Do the benefits justify those costs?
- What should I do personally right now to preserve evidence and avoid spoliation? (save emails, preserve devices, avoid deleting files, notes about conversations)

Short email template to your attorney (Robert Kim)
